// An INFERRED nullable return whose non-null arm is a LIST (or a closure).
//
//   emitProgram: bare null needs a struct-typed context           (i32[])
//   'f' infers the nullable return type string[] | null — …       (string[], at CHECK)
//
// silent-class inventory D1062. ONE shape, TWO channels: the `i32[]` element type reaches
// the emitter and refuses there, the `string[]` one is refused by the checker with a
// message that concedes the program is type-valid. Both are clause-2 violations.
//
// ANNOTATING THE RETURN FIXES IT, which is exactly why a fixture that annotates every
// destination cannot see this defect (D969's rule). The annotated twin
// `function f(n: i32): i32[] | null` runs and prints `false`.
//
// Arms that already RUN under inference: `i32`, `string`, a struct, and a MAP. Arm order
// does not matter — `return null` first refuses identically.
//
// Should print false then true.
function f(n: i32) {
  if n == 0 { return [1, 2] }
  return null
}

print(f(0) == null)
print(f(1) == null)
